From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 12:07:02 +0000
Subject: net: ethernet: lantiq: complete the GSW-R switch-core init

AVM's legacy_switch_core_init makes five register writes that it gates on
sdev == LTQ_FLOW_DEV_INT_R alone - not on the presence of a WAN port - so
they apply to this board, whose GSW-R carries no WAN netdev but does sit in
the live host-RX path via the DMA1TX<->DMA2RX peer-to-peer bridge. Our port
carried none of them. All five register definitions were already present in
gsw_reg.h; only the writes were missing.

  MAC_FLEN.LEN     = 1612  gsw_flow_core.c:2815-2817
  PMAC_CTRL_2.MLEN = 1     :2818-2820
  MAC_CTRL_2.MLEN  = 1     :2821-2822
  PCE_TFCR_NUM.NUM = 0x80  :2886-2887
  PMAC_CTRL_0.PADEN = 1    :2800-2802

The first three are one group, AVM's "JZ-33918: enable jumbo frames up
to mtu 1590 on the GSWIP-R", where 1612 is 1590 + VLAN_ETH_HLEN +
ETH_FCS_LEN. Our only MAC_FLEN write was explicitly excluded for GSW-R,
and the matching PMAC_CTRL_2.MLEN = 0 lives in gsw_pmac_glbl_cfg, which
GSW-R does not call - so GSW-R ran with MAC_FLEN at silicon reset and
both long-frame enables at zero. It gets AVM's else-arm now, written as
the else-arm of the same condition so the two arms read the way AVM's
do.

This does not change any netdev MTU: ndo_change_mtu remains the
deliberate no-op it is, and the interfaces stay at ETH_DATA_LEN. It
stops GSW-R's frame budget from being the binding constraint underneath
them, which matters for full-size VLAN-tagged frames crossing the bridge
on the way to the CPU.

PCE_TFCR_NUM sizes the PCE flow-engine common region at 128 entries. The
acronym is not expanded anywhere in the 4.9 source, but the register titles
in gsw_reg.h settle what it is - 0x46E "PCE Flow Engine Common Region Start
Index", 0x46F its "Entry Numbers" - a flow-table region descriptor, not a
MAC transmit flow-control register. AVM writes NUM at three GSW-R-gated
sites and never writes PCE_TFCR_ID; this follows suit, and goes at the tail
of the GSWIP-3.0 RMON block where AVM's first site sits.

PMAC_CTRL_0.PADEN = 1 enables padding to 64 bytes. It is a raw gsw_w32
in AVM's GSW-R arm, and the reason we missed it is a false negative in
our own comment on gsw_pmac_ig_cfg_r: "AVM issues NO PMAC global-cfg for
GSW-R". True of GSW_PMAC_Glbl_CfgSet, which AVM really never calls for
GSW-R - but it reads as though the whole PMAC global block were
untouched, and it is not. That comment is corrected here rather than
left to mislead the next audit. AVM writes MAC_CTRL_0.PADEN (0x903) on
neither instance, so nor do we.

The invariant this change is built around: the GSW-L register sequence
is byte-identical before and after. Every one of the five writes is
gated on the GSW-R device - three in a new else-arm, one behind an sdev
test, one inside the existing GSW-R-only PMAC branch - and the GSW-L
MAC_FLEN = 1518 write is unchanged but for the braces now around it.
GSW-R is the live CPU RX path, so this is the highest per-line risk in
the phase and wants its own A/B against a build without it.

MAC_CTRL_2 @ 0x905 carries an unresolved MAC-index 0-vs-1 ambiguity: the
gsw_reg.h base arithmetic reads as index 0, while AVM's GSW-L runtime
formula implies a -1 base, and there is no hardware reference manual to
arbitrate. It is written with the literal base exactly as AVM writes it.
Do not "correct" the arithmetic on reasoning alone.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
index c94f6ec96d84..c6dc469d30ed 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_flow_pce.c
@@ -849,6 +849,11 @@ int gsw_pmicro_code_init(void *cdev)
 				BM_RMON_CTRL_BCAST_CNT_SHIFT,
 				BM_RMON_CTRL_BCAST_CNT_SIZE, 1);
 		}
+
+		if (gswdev->sdev == LTQ_FLOW_DEV_INT_R)
+			gsw_w32(cdev, PCE_TFCR_NUM_NUM_OFFSET,
+				PCE_TFCR_NUM_NUM_SHIFT,
+				PCE_TFCR_NUM_NUM_SIZE, 0x80);
 	}
 
 	/*
@@ -942,16 +947,26 @@ int gsw_pmicro_code_init(void *cdev)
 	gsw_w32(cdev, MAC_PFSA_2_PFAD_OFFSET, MAC_PFSA_2_PFAD_SHIFT,
 		MAC_PFSA_2_PFAD_SIZE, 0xAC9A);
 
-	if (gswdev->sdev != LTQ_FLOW_DEV_INT_R)
+	if (gswdev->sdev != LTQ_FLOW_DEV_INT_R) {
+		/* "discard jumbo frames on the GSWIP-L" (AVM :2823-2830) */
 		gsw_w32(cdev, MAC_FLEN_LEN_OFFSET, MAC_FLEN_LEN_SHIFT,
 			MAC_FLEN_LEN_SIZE, 1518);
-
+	} else {
+		gsw_w32(cdev, MAC_FLEN_LEN_OFFSET, MAC_FLEN_LEN_SHIFT,
+			MAC_FLEN_LEN_SIZE, 1612);
+		gsw_w32(cdev, PMAC_CTRL_2_MLEN_OFFSET, PMAC_CTRL_2_MLEN_SHIFT,
+			PMAC_CTRL_2_MLEN_SIZE, 1);
+		gsw_w32(cdev, MAC_CTRL_2_MLEN_OFFSET, MAC_CTRL_2_MLEN_SHIFT,
+			MAC_CTRL_2_MLEN_SIZE, 1);
+	}
 
 	if (gswdev->sdev == LTQ_FLOW_DEV_INT) {
 		gsw_pmac_glbl_cfg(cdev);
 		gsw_pmac_ig_cfg(cdev);
 		gsw_pmac_eg_cfg(cdev);
 	} else if (gswdev->sdev == LTQ_FLOW_DEV_INT_R) {
+		gsw_w32(cdev, PMAC_CTRL_0_PADEN_OFFSET, PMAC_CTRL_0_PADEN_SHIFT,
+			PMAC_CTRL_0_PADEN_SIZE, 1);
 		gsw_pmac_ig_cfg_r(cdev);
 		gsw_pmac_eg_cfg_r(cdev);
 	}
